Subject: [PATCH] a mage never conjures food or water, because nothing ever asks it to

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS

The roster's mage has known how to conjure food since level 6 and water since
level 4. He is level 26. He has never done it once. Measured, not inferred:

  * The five roster characters carry, between them, ZERO items whose
    item_template.spellcategory_1 is 11 (food) or 59 (drink). That is the
    game's own classification and it is the same one this module's upstream
    uses (InventoryAction.cpp:243 and :250 build a FindFoodVisitor on
    categories 11 and 59). Not "not much". None.
  * Nothing conjured exists anywhere in those bags either: zero consumables
    carrying ITEM_FLAG_CONJURED, which is the flag every Conjured * item has
    and the one the "conjured food" inventory qualifier tests.
  * The mage's own spell rows carry 587, 597, 990 (Conjure Food ranks 1 to 3)
    and 5504, 5505, 5506 (Conjure Water ranks 1 to 3), all active.
  * His live non-combat strategy set contains "food", so the strategy that
    would EAT food is on. There has simply never been any food.

Over one measured evening the party died seven times in twenty one minutes.
Two of the five are mana users who cannot restore mana between pulls without
something to drink, and the mage who would supply it is one of them.

WHERE IT ACTUALLY BREAKS, AND IT IS ONE LINE OF ABSENCE

Both ends of this feature are already in this tree. Neither end is wired to
the other.

  * The TRIGGERS exist. NoFoodTrigger and NoDrinkTrigger are declared in
    src/Ai/Base/Trigger/GenericTriggers.h:276 and :284, defined in
    GenericTriggers.cpp:257 and :266, and registered by name as "no food" and
    "no drink" in src/Ai/Base/TriggerContext.h:144-145. Their condition is
    specifically written for a conjurer: `AI_VALUE2(std::vector<Item*>,
    "inventory items", "conjured food").empty()`. They ask "do you have any
    CONJURED food", which is a question only a class that can conjure has any
    reason to be asked.

  * The ACTIONS exist. CastConjureFoodAction and CastConjureWaterAction are
    declared in src/Ai/Class/Mage/MageActions.h:167 and :173 and registered as
    "conjure food" and "conjure water" in MageAiObjectContext.cpp:197-198.
    There is even a bespoke branch in CastSpellAction::Execute
    (GenericSpellActions.cpp:141) that exists only for these two names and
    picks the highest known rank whose product the bot can actually use.

  * NOTHING PUSHES A TriggerNode FOR EITHER. Grepping the whole tree for
    `NextAction("conjure` returns exactly one hit, and it is "conjure mana
    gem" in this file. Grepping for a TriggerNode naming "no food" or "no
    drink" returns none at all. Both triggers and both actions are reachable
    only by name, and no strategy in the tree ever names them.

So the mage does not conjure because it is never asked, and this file is where
the asking would go: GenericMageNonCombatStrategy is the mage's non-combat
strategy and already wires the third conjure ("no mana gem" -> "conjure mana
gem", one line above the two added here). The two added lines are the same
shape as that one, using triggers and actions this tree already ships and
already registers.

WHY THESE RELEVANCE NUMBERS. Below the three buffs this strategy already
carries (arcane intellect 21, conjure mana gem 20, focus magic 19) and above
the oil (1). A mage with no armour buff and no food should put the armour up
first; the food is worth doing before nothing at all. Water is one below food
because a mage out of both wants the thing that also restores health first.

WHAT THIS PATCH DELIBERATELY DOES NOT DO

  * It does not change the trigger's threshold. NoFoodTrigger fires when the
    conjured stack is EMPTY, so this makes a mage keep itself supplied and
    nothing more. Conjuring a surplus for four other characters is a decision
    about a party, taken from outside, and mod-overseer's kind='conjure' row
    is where that decision lives. A trigger that tried to hold a party's worth
    of stock would be this module guessing at a party size it is not told.

  * It does not touch the dead branch it found. CastConjureFoodAction derives
    from CastBuffSpellAction, whose Execute override (GenericSpellActions.cpp:
    287) calls botAI->CastSpell(spell, target) directly, so the conjure branch
    in CastSpellAction::Execute is unreachable from these two actions. The
    name path resolves to the highest known rank anyway (SpellIdValue), so the
    behaviour is right; the branch is simply dead code. Noted rather than
    changed, because a base-class change here would alter GetTargetName for
    both actions and is a second, separable fix.

  * It adds no new trigger, action, value or strategy. Two lines, both of the
    form this file already uses, naming four things that were already here.

WHEN THIS PATCH SHOULD BE DELETED. The moment upstream pushes either
TriggerNode into any mage strategy. apply-patches.sh will fail the build the
day that happens, which is the intended way to find out.

diff --git a/src/Ai/Class/Mage/Strategy/GenericMageNonCombatStrategy.cpp b/src/Ai/Class/Mage/Strategy/GenericMageNonCombatStrategy.cpp
index 574302fb..14faf1a5 100644
--- a/src/Ai/Class/Mage/Strategy/GenericMageNonCombatStrategy.cpp
+++ b/src/Ai/Class/Mage/Strategy/GenericMageNonCombatStrategy.cpp
@@ -39,6 +39,8 @@ void GenericMageNonCombatStrategy::InitTriggers(std::vector<TriggerNode*>& trigg
     triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("no focus magic", { NextAction("focus magic on party", 19.0f) }));
     triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("often", { NextAction("apply oil", 1.0f) }));
     triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("no mana gem", { NextAction("conjure mana gem", 20.0f) }));
+    triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("no food", { NextAction("conjure food", 18.0f) }));
+    triggers.push_back(new TriggerNode("no drink", { NextAction("conjure water", 17.0f) }));
 }
 
 void MageBuffManaStrategy::InitTriggers(std::vector<TriggerNode*>& triggers)
